{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery-class/class-settle-v1-1.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"question\": {\n  \"question\": \"v1\",\n  \"about\": \"NVDA\",\n  \"kind\": \"asset\",\n  \"asks\": \"What total amount in USD of NVIDIA’s credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees or backstops to AI labs and cloud partners, including the support for the additional lab’s nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity, will be disclosed in NVIDIA’s FY2027 Form 10-K and related quantified disclosures by the annual report’s publication?\",\n  \"settles_on\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n  \"due\": \"2027-04-01\",\n  \"possible_answers\": [\n   {\n    \"answer\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bullish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"below 10,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Bullish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 10,000,000,000 USD to below 25,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 3,\n    \"label\": \"Neutral\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 25,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened)\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 4,\n    \"label\": \"Bearish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"answer\": 5,\n    \"label\": \"Strongly bearish\",\n    \"holds_when\": \"100,000,000,000 USD or more\"\n   }\n  ]\n },\n \"what_the_judge_was_shown\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount in USD of NVIDIA’s credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees or backstops to AI labs and cloud partners, including the support for the additional lab’s nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity, will be disclosed in NVIDIA’s FY2027 Form 10-K and related quantified disclosures by the annual report’s publication?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 10,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 10,000,000,000 USD to below 25,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 25,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 100,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 37 items]\\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\n- e38 [fact; Financial Times (reported via Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy 2026-09-22; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy, the SoftBank-backed developer, slowed preparations for a planned public listing that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\\n    Quote: SB Energy's planned public listing is turning into a stress test for the financing side of the AI buildout. The SoftBank-backed developer has slowed preparations for an IPO that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package has faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e79 [third_party_view; Morningstar article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f0206f98cb85dd776005198 2026-09-23] Morningstar, listing the year's major Nvidia news in September 2026, describes Nvidia's \\\"role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments\\\" as one of the threads around which its dividend announcement was overlooked. The article does not specify the form of that financing (equity, debt, credit support or guarantees), the counterparties, or the period covered.\\n    Quote: Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e118, e144, e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e92 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] AI cloud provider Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on 18 September 2026 disclosing that it lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on revenue of just $140.6 million — the financial condition of the counterparty to which NVIDIA is committing $1 billion of convertible financing.\\n    Quote: Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on September 18 with one number that makes the AI-cloud boom look radioactive: the company lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on just $140.6 million of revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e194; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e94 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that as of 31 August 2026 it had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years, operating in 14 regions with more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity — the contracted demand base underlying the Nscale build-out NVIDIA supplies and is financing.\\n    Quote: As of August 31, Nscale had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts, with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years. It operates in 14 regions and has more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity.\\n- e95 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that a single customer generated 52% of its first-half 2026 revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic, indicating concentrated demand behind its contract book.\\n    Quote: Nscale disclosed that one customer generated 52% of first-half revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic.\\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \\\"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e193 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing the Financial Times and Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale, the Nvidia-backed 'neocloud' headquartered in London, filed for a New York listing in the week before 24 September 2026 at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times; it is asking investors to value it at as much as that on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: This time it’s Nscale, the Nvidia-backed “neocloud” that filed for a New York listing last week at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times . … Despite those losses, Nscale is asking Wall Street to value it at as much as $35 billion on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\\n- e194 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's S-1 reported a net loss of $1.02 billion for the six months to June 30, 2026, and at one point the company's own management flagged 'substantial doubt' about its ability to continue as a going concern.\\n    Quote: Per the S-1, the company posted a net loss of $1.02 billion in the six months to June 30, 2026—and at one point, its own management flagged “substantial doubt” about its ability to continue as a going concern.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e195 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale 2026-09-15; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] On Sept. 15, 2026, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 15, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n- e200 [fact; BioSpace, citing Basecamp Research's news release; original source Basecamp Research (issuer) and NVIDIA (participating investor) 2026-09-23; from gnews:c20b8b53b03e35fcff0718ce 2026-09-24] Basecamp Research closed a $140 million series C round, announced in a news release on Wednesday, in which NVIDIA participated as an investor; the oversubscribed round was led by venture capital firm S32. The item does not disclose the size of NVIDIA's individual investment.\\n    Quote: Basecamp Research has closed a $140 million series C fundraising to advance its pipeline of therapies designed using machine learning models. … The London- and Boston-based company is backed by NVIDIA, which participated in the oversubscribed round that was led by the venture capital firm S32, according to a news release on Wednesday .\\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\\n- e237 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, and management concedes some will call this circular financing while saying the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\n    Quote: Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e273 [fact; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e274 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028; Trefis notes that part of the business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\n    Quote: Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\\n- e329 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic is reportedly seeking to raise as much as $100 billion in an IPO that might value it at roughly $2 trillion, and Reuters has since said the listing may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November; the article says the plans may change and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n    Quote: The artificial intelligence (AI) company behind the Claude models is reportedly looking to raise as much as $100 billion in a listing that might value it at roughly $2 trillion. Reuters has since said that the IPO may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November. Of course, the plans might still change, and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e372 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, which the article describes as another large financing round for an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity and as evidence that GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure for the AI cloud operator.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, adding another large financing round to an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity. CoreWeave’s latest convertible financing showed how quickly GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure.\\n- e373 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nebius (as reported by cryptorank.io) ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] The article reports that Nebius has also turned to billions in financing and that the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt.\\n    Quote: The same issue extends across the sector. Nebius has also turned to billions in financing , while the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt .\\n- e374 [fact; cryptorank.io citing Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Bond investors are noticing. Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require.\\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held talks with insurance companies over ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as the chipmaker seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, the Financial Times reported on Tuesday. … The discussions include potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, which could protect lenders if a borrower defaults and N\\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, while people familiar with the discussions said the company was also working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers. Howden declined to comment, the report said. … The potential structures could extend beyond traditional insurers. Nvidia has explored using insura\\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 3]\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) By January 31, 2027, will U.S. authorities authorize Nvidia to sell Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and will Chinese authorities permit those shipments?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s outlook: it excludes China data-center compute revenue, which is closest to rung 3, Still open; it is not a forecast of a formal prohibition. The evidence supports some route to Chinese purchases of the Blackwell-generation RTX PRO 5500, but the reported Chinese intent is u\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026, as shown by their Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 775,000,000,000 USD to below 825,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-28; reasons: The reference is nearly $800 billion, which sits in rung 3. The direct forecast still implies about $800 billion for the five in calendar 2026, within rung 3's $775 billion–below-$825 billion range. Alphabet's $200 billion is a component of the aggregate, not an additional amount; the GPU and capaci\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The available reference is no explicit consensus probability; the reported expectation that MIIT intends to approve some purchase plans, and that some firms were told approval is likely, places the reference at rung 1. I also forecast rung 1: those reports are specific to the firms and product in th\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the ladder’s no-reference baseline, rung 3 ($25bn to below $50bn). The strongest quantified evidence is a current maximum guarantee-obligation cap of $108.5bn, with the $105bn OpenAI lease guarantee treated as an example within that exposure, not added on top. That implies an amount above the $100bn rung-5 boundary. The newly quantified Nscale lease guarantee and the additional capacity and Aolani support add context, but there is no evidence that these sit outside the aggregate cap or a disclosed amount with which to add them. The other large financing and equity figures are not themselves credit-support commitments or minimum-revenue guarantees.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move\",\n   \"text\": \"NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 94,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"techrepublic.com\",\n   \"title\": \"22,000 Nvidia Blackwell GPUs Are Headed to Malaysia and the Philippines in 2027\",\n   \"text\": \"Aolani plans 22,000 Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs across Malaysia and the Philippines as Southeast Asia’s AI infrastructure buildout accelerates.\\nSoutheast Asia’s AI compute race is set to add 22,000 more Blackwell Ultra GPUs.\\nSingapore-founded AI cloud provider Aolani said Sept. 21 that it plans to deploy 22,000 Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs across two AI factories in Malaysia and the Philippines in early 2027. The expansion would take Aolani’s total GPU deployment above 48,000 and its AI factory capacity past 100 MW.\\nFor enterprises evaluating regional AI capacity, the plan would add a sizable pool of accelerated compute. In its strategic compute collaboration with Nvidia , Aolani did not disclose the exact sites, country-by-country GPU allocation, project cost, or customer availability timeline.\\nAolani sets early 2027 Blackwell rollout\\nAolani plans to use the systems for cloud services supporting AI training, inference, and other compute-intensive workloads for enterprises, startups, developers, and institutions across Southeast Asia.\\nNvidia’s Blackwell Ultra platform is built for large-scale AI reasoning and inference workloads. Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\\nMalaysia already hosts Nvidia-backed AI infrastructure. SNS Network has launched an AI Factory at Telekom Malaysia’s Klang Valley Data Center in Cyberjaya, while OpenAI has agreed to anchor two planned Firmus AI Factory sites in Malaysia . The projects add to the country’s growing AI-compute footprint.\\nThe Philippines is still building out large-scale accelerated-compute infrastructure. Aolani says its planned facility would be among the country’s first large-scale AI infrastructure platforms. The government also released a $34.4 billion AI infrastructure plan in September targeting 1.5 GW of data center capacity by 2033.\\nMalaysia and the Philippines join a wider GPU buildout\\nAolani says the deployment would bring its total GPU count above 48,000 and AI factory capacity above 100 MW. Those are projected totals tied to the expansion, not capacity already operating in Malaysia and the Philippines.\\nThe project joins a wider wave of regional investment. More than $50 billion has been invested by AWS, Google, and Microsoft in AI-ready data center and cloud infrastructure across Southeast Asia, according to research from Singapore’s Economic Development Board and McKinsey.\\nIndonesia is drawing similar capital. Zankore secured up to $3.1 billion in financing in September for Nvidia-powered AI infrastructure, another sign of accelerated-computing investment spreading across Southeast Asia.\\nKey details of Aolani’s project remain undisclosed, including the GPU split between the two countries, exact facility locations, pricing, project cost, and customer commitments. Deployment is expected in early 2027, but Aolani has not said when customers will be able to buy the additional capacity.\\nFor APAC IT leaders, the next milestones are the site locations, commercial availability, and how much of the 22,000-GPU deployment lands in each market.\\nMeta’s Ray-Ban and Oakley AI glasses are now available in Malaysia. Compare four models, their prices, features, and prescription compatibility.\\nWorkato opens AI agent building in Singapore with 50,000 free starting credits, giving Asian developers and businesses access to automation tools.\\nApple is restricting iPhone Duo walk-in sales in Japan, South Korea, and the UAE ahead of its Oct. 23 launch. Here’s what buyers should know before visiting.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\",\n   \"text\": \"NVIDIA Stock Looks Expensive Until You Price The Vera Rubin Ramp\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) stock trades near $225, about 28.3 times its adjusted earnings over the past twelve months. Adjusted here means normalized net income with stock-based compensation added back, the same broad basis analysts use in their forecasts. For a stock near its 52-week high, that looks steep. But the multiple leaves out the growth analysts already expect, and that growth depends on how much NVIDIA can supply.\\nNVIDIA Looks Ordinary Once The Forecast Earnings Arrive\\nOn the earnings analysts expect for fiscal 2027, the same price is about 24.1 times. On what they expect for fiscal 2028, it is about 14.5 times. The price is the same. The earnings analysts expect it to buy are not.\\nNVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell. Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\nSo What Stands Between NVIDIA And Those Earnings?\\nFor fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there. The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme.\\nSome of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\nAnalysts also disagree on what that growth earns. The 33 estimates of fiscal 2028 earnings run from $12.62 to $17.49 a share, a wide band for the year the forward multiple is priced on.\\nAnd NVIDIA’s Gross Margin Is Guided Lower As Memory Costs Climb\\nConsensus has earnings and revenue growing at a similar pace between fiscal 2027 and fiscal 2028, so analysts assume profit margins hold roughly steady. Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\nFor the forecast to hold, that lost gross margin has to be made up further down the income statement. That is the second condition behind the fiscal 2028 multiple, alongside supply.\\nSo Is NVIDIA Cheap, Or Only If The Chips Get Built?\\nOnly if the chips get built, which means the supply arrives and the margins hold. The trailing multiple cannot tell you either. How much would you stake on a year that has not started?\\nChecking that for every stock you own is a job most people have no time for. Since its inception, our rule-based Trefis High Quality Portfolio has outperformed its benchmark, a blend of three major indices.\\nOr hunt for these yourself: our Forward Valuation Discount screen ranks stocks priced furthest below their expected earnings. It shows where to look, and the forecast still has to come true.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 165,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Elon Musk Just Gave Nvidia Investors More Good News. Is the Stock a Buy?\",\n   \"text\": \"Elon Musk has been one of the biggest sources of good news for Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) investors in recent months, and that continued on Friday (Sept. 25). In a post on his \\\"X\\\" platform, formerly Twitter, Musk gave an update on Colossus 2, SpaceX's ( SPCX +0.63% ) xAI unit's artificial intelligence (AI) computing cluster. He said the massive AI training supercomputer could double the number of Nvidia graphics processing units (GPUs) deployed by year-end.\\nIn the post, Musk noted that Colossus 2 currently has 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s. An additional 220,000 GB300 chips are set to come online next week, with another 220,000 GPUs set for November. He added that he hopes to get another 220,000 GB300s in late December, \\\"if we get lucky.\\\"\\nSpaceX's original goal was to scale to 1 million GPUs by the end of 2026, so it looks like it will surpass this if the orders are completed. According to reports, xAI spent around $18 billion on its initial 550,000 GPUs. This order could be worth even more given that it consists of more of its newer GB300 GPUs.\\nNvidia and SpaceX are becoming close partners\\nThis update follows comments from Elon Musk earlier this month that SpaceX would exclusively use Nvidia chips moving forward. The Tesla and SpaceX CEO called Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform the best architecture out there and said it valued its partnership with the chipmaker. At the time, Musk said SpaceX would end the year with around 2 gigawatts of computing power, while it is looking to end 2027 with closer to 10 gigawatts than 5 gigawatts. One gigawatt of compute power costs about $50 billion to $60 billion, with chips and other Nvidia components making up about $35 billion of that total cost.\\nIn addition to helping expand xAI's computing capacity, Nvidia and SpaceX also announced a partnership to develop GPUs for the company's planned future orbital data centers. Cosmic radiation can affect current chips, flipping bits from a 0 to a 1 or vice versa, causing significant computational errors. Worst of all, there is currently no way to flip it back, and it's difficult to detect without a redundant system to check it against. In a post on X, Musk said the two companies have designed a Vera Rubin NVL72 system optimized for use in space. It plans to launch the system into orbit toward the end of 2027 and then start scaling it in 2028.\\nIs Nvidia stock a buy?\\nSpaceX has some of the biggest ambitions in the entire AI sector , so having it as an anchor customer that exclusively uses its chips is a major coup for Nvidia. And of course, Nvidia's strengths extend well beyond SpaceX and its budding relationship with Musk.\\nWhile competition is increasing, particularly in the inference market, Nvidia remains the dominant player in AI infrastructure. Its GPUs are the primary chips used in AI model training, and its CUDA software platform still provides a wide moat in this area. Meanwhile, the company's acquisition of \\\"Groq\\\" and its language processing unit (LPU) technology, which it has incorporated into its CUDA ecosystem, gives it a high-end differentiated approach to the fast-growing inference market.\\nIts ARM -based Vera CPUs are also gaining strong traction with agentic AI. In August, Nvidia announced that SpaceX will use its Vera central processing units (CPUs) for its agentic AI workloads. While the CPU market isn't nearly as large as the GPU market, this is still another nice win.\\nNvidia is no longer just a GPU company, but a complete AI infrastructure provider that is also starting to stack on some important software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face (pending). With the stock trading at a forward price-to-earnings ratio of just 14 times fiscal 2028 (ending January 2028), Nvidia is a buy.\\nBy Geoffrey Seiler – Sep 27, 2026 at 8:00PM EST\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 203,\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Drops Massive Number on Anthropic AI Spending\",\n   \"text\": \"Anthropics artificial-intelligence infrastructure commitments are becoming enormous, with Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) saying the AI developer now has more than $180 billion of contracted value across cloud providers and neocloud operators .\\nNvidia disclosed the figure during its latest Non-Deal Roadshow, offering a new look at just how aggressively Anthropic is locking in computing capacity as it competes with OpenAI and other frontier-model developers.\\nThe Dario Amodei-led company has also contracted for 2.5 gigawatts of Nvidia AI infrastructure scheduled for delivery through 2028.\\nThat is important for Nvidia because each gigawatt of AI infrastructure is becoming substantially more valuable to the chipmaker.\\nNvidia said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin .\\nNvidia continues to increase its share within the overall AI infrastructure market, the company said.\\nThe increase reflects more than higher-end GPUs. Nvidia is expanding into CPU racks for agentic AI, low-latency processing systems, networking and other infrastructure needed to operate what it describes as full-stack AI factories.\\nIts share of information-technology capital spending among the five largest cloud service providers has climbed from 11% in the first quarter of 2023 and is projected by Nvidia to reach 44% by 2027 .\\nNeoclouds are becoming another part of that expansion.\\nNvidia said Australian partners including IREN (IREN) and Firmus expect to bring roughly 2 gigawatts of capacity online by 2027 .\\nThe $180 billion figure is striking, but investors should distinguish Anthropics total contracted cloud and neocloud value from revenue going directly to Nvidia .\\nThe stronger Nvidia signal may instead be the combination of Anthropics 2.5GW commitment and Nvidias rapidly rising revenue opportunity per gigawatt.\\nIf Rubin-era systems really generate roughly $40 billion of opportunity per gigawatt, continued capacity commitments from frontier AI developers could dramatically expand Nvidias addressable market.\\nThe metrics to watch are therefore contracted gigawatts, Rubin deployments, cloud-provider capital spending and how much of each AI data center Nvidia captures beyond GPUs.\\nThat broader infrastructure opportunity helps explain why Nvidia simultaneously expanded its share-repurchase authorization by $150 billion to $235 billion while continuing to invest aggressively in AI.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"items\": [\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:e113\",\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:e115\",\n   \"article\": 46,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"The article reports that NVIDIA's days sales outstanding (DSO) is extending to 60 days, listing it among the company's real risks.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:e219\",\n   \"article\": 94,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"said_by\": \"Aolani\",\n   \"summary\": \"Aolani, a Singapore-founded AI cloud provider, said on 21 September 2026 that it plans to deploy 22,000 Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs across two AI factories in Malaysia and the Philippines in early 2027, a plan that would take its total GPU deployment above 48,000 and its AI factory capacity past 100 MW; Aolani did not disclose the exact sites, the country-by-country GPU allocation, project cost or the customer availability timeline, and the capacity totals are projected, not operating.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Singapore-founded AI cloud provider Aolani said Sept. 21 that it plans to deploy 22,000 Nvidia Blackwell Ultra GPUs across two AI factories in Malaysia and the Philippines in early 2027. The expansion would take Aolani’s total GPU deployment above 48,000 and its AI factory capacity past 100 MW. … In its strategic compute collaboration with Nvidia , Aolani did not disclose the exact sites, country-by-country GPU allocation, project cost, or customer availability timeline.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:e233\",\n   \"article\": 98,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA management\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management said Vera Rubin, its newest platform, is expected to be the fastest product ramp in the company's history, and put NVIDIA's revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin versus about $25 billion per gigawatt with Blackwell.\",\n   \"quote\": \"NVIDIA expects Vera Rubin, its newest platform, to be the fastest product ramp in the company’s history. Management puts NVIDIA’s revenue opportunity at about $40 billion per gigawatt of capacity with Vera Rubin, against $25 billion with Blackwell.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:e389\",\n   \"article\": 165,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n   \"said_by\": \"fool.com / Geoffrey Seiler\",\n   \"summary\": \"The article estimates that one gigawatt of computing power costs about $50 billion to $60 billion, with chips and other Nvidia components making up about $35 billion of that total cost.\",\n   \"quote\": \"One gigawatt of compute power costs about $50 billion to $60 billion, with chips and other Nvidia components making up about $35 billion of that total cost.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"item\": \"v1:e488\",\n   \"article\": 203,\n   \"article_day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n   \"said_by\": \"NVIDIA\",\n   \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin.\",\n   \"quote\": \"Nvidia said its revenue opportunity per gigawatt, based on its reference designs, has risen from roughly $18 billion during the Hopper generation to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Rubin .\"\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery-class/class-settle-v1-1.json\\\")); print(p[\\\"what_the_judge_was_shown\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/delivery-class/class-settle-v1-1.json\\\")); print(p[\\\"what_the_judge_was_shown\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount in USD of NVIDIA’s credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees or backstops to AI labs and cloud partners, including the support for the additional lab’s nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity, will be disclosed in NVIDIA’s FY2027 Form 10-K and related quantified disclosures by the annual report’s publication?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 10,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 10,000,000,000 USD to below 25,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 25,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 100,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 37 items]\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n- e38 [fact; Financial Times (reported via Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy 2026-09-22; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy, the SoftBank-backed developer, slowed preparations for a planned public listing that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\n    Quote: SB Energy's planned public listing is turning into a stress test for the financing side of the AI buildout. The SoftBank-backed developer has slowed preparations for an IPO that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package has faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e79 [third_party_view; Morningstar article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f0206f98cb85dd776005198 2026-09-23] Morningstar, listing the year's major Nvidia news in September 2026, describes Nvidia's \"role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments\" as one of the threads around which its dividend announcement was overlooked. The article does not specify the form of that financing (equity, debt, credit support or guarantees), the counterparties, or the period covered.\n    Quote: Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e118, e144, e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e92 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] AI cloud provider Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on 18 September 2026 disclosing that it lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on revenue of just $140.6 million — the financial condition of the counterparty to which NVIDIA is committing $1 billion of convertible financing.\n    Quote: Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on September 18 with one number that makes the AI-cloud boom look radioactive: the company lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on just $140.6 million of revenue.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e194; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e94 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that as of 31 August 2026 it had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years, operating in 14 regions with more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity — the contracted demand base underlying the Nscale build-out NVIDIA supplies and is financing.\n    Quote: As of August 31, Nscale had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts, with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years. It operates in 14 regions and has more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity.\n- e95 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that a single customer generated 52% of its first-half 2026 revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic, indicating concentrated demand behind its contract book.\n    Quote: Nscale disclosed that one customer generated 52% of first-half revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e193 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing the Financial Times and Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale, the Nvidia-backed 'neocloud' headquartered in London, filed for a New York listing in the week before 24 September 2026 at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times; it is asking investors to value it at as much as that on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\n    Quote: This time it’s Nscale, the Nvidia-backed “neocloud” that filed for a New York listing last week at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times . … Despite those losses, Nscale is asking Wall Street to value it at as much as $35 billion on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\n- e194 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's S-1 reported a net loss of $1.02 billion for the six months to June 30, 2026, and at one point the company's own management flagged 'substantial doubt' about its ability to continue as a going concern.\n    Quote: Per the S-1, the company posted a net loss of $1.02 billion in the six months to June 30, 2026—and at one point, its own management flagged “substantial doubt” about its ability to continue as a going concern.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e195 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale 2026-09-15; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] On Sept. 15, 2026, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\n    Quote: On Sept. 15, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n- e200 [fact; BioSpace, citing Basecamp Research's news release; original source Basecamp Research (issuer) and NVIDIA (participating investor) 2026-09-23; from gnews:c20b8b53b03e35fcff0718ce 2026-09-24] Basecamp Research closed a $140 million series C round, announced in a news release on Wednesday, in which NVIDIA participated as an investor; the oversubscribed round was led by venture capital firm S32. The item does not disclose the size of NVIDIA's individual investment.\n    Quote: Basecamp Research has closed a $140 million series C fundraising to advance its pipeline of therapies designed using machine learning models. … The London- and Boston-based company is backed by NVIDIA, which participated in the oversubscribed round that was led by the venture capital firm S32, according to a news release on Wednesday .\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n- e237 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, and management concedes some will call this circular financing while saying the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\n    Quote: Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e273 [fact; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e274 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028; Trefis notes that part of the business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\n    Quote: Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\n- e329 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic is reportedly seeking to raise as much as $100 billion in an IPO that might value it at roughly $2 trillion, and Reuters has since said the listing may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November; the article says the plans may change and neither company has confirmed the talks.\n    Quote: The artificial intelligence (AI) company behind the Claude models is reportedly looking to raise as much as $100 billion in a listing that might value it at roughly $2 trillion. Reuters has since said that the IPO may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November. Of course, the plans might still change, and neither company has confirmed the talks.\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e372 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, which the article describes as another large financing round for an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity and as evidence that GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure for the AI cloud operator.\n    Quote: CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, adding another large financing round to an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity. CoreWeave’s latest convertible financing showed how quickly GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure.\n- e373 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nebius (as reported by cryptorank.io) ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] The article reports that Nebius has also turned to billions in financing and that the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt.\n    Quote: The same issue extends across the sector. Nebius has also turned to billions in financing , while the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt .\n- e374 [fact; cryptorank.io citing Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require, according to the article.\n    Quote: Bond investors are noticing. Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require.\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \"neocloud\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held talks with insurance companies over ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as the chipmaker seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, the Financial Times reported on Tuesday. … The discussions include potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \"neocloud\" companies, which could protect lenders if a borrower defaults and N\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\n    Quote: Nvidia has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, while people familiar with the discussions said the company was also working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers. Howden declined to comment, the report said. … The potential structures could extend beyond traditional insurers. Nvidia has explored using insura\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n\n[Themes in force, 3]\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) By January 31, 2027, will U.S. authorities authorize Nvidia to sell Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and will Chinese authorities permit those shipments?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s outlook: it excludes China data-center compute revenue, which is closest to rung 3, Still open; it is not a forecast of a formal prohibition. The evidence supports some route to Chinese purchases of the Blackwell-generation RTX PRO 5500, but the reported Chinese intent is u\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026, as shown by their Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures?: now rung 3 from 775,000,000,000 USD to below 825,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-28; reasons: The reference is nearly $800 billion, which sits in rung 3. The direct forecast still implies about $800 billion for the five in calendar 2026, within rung 3's $775 billion–below-$825 billion range. Alphabet's $200 billion is a component of the aggregate, not an additional amount; the GPU and capaci\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-28; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The available reference is no explicit consensus probability; the reported expectation that MIIT intends to approve some purchase plans, and that some firms were told approval is likely, places the reference at rung 1. I also forecast rung 1: those reports are specific to the firms and product in th\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the ladder’s no-reference baseline, rung 3 ($25bn to below $50bn). The strongest quantified evidence is a current maximum guarantee-obligation cap of $108.5bn, with the $105bn OpenAI lease guarantee treated as an example within that exposure, not added on top. That implies an amount above the $100bn rung-5 boundary. The newly quantified Nscale lease guarantee and the additional capacity and Aolani support add context, but there is no evidence that these sit outside the aggregate cap or a disclosed amount with which to add them. The other large financing and equity figures are not themselves credit-support commitments or minimum-revenue guarantees.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item\":\"v1:e113\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA has $279 billion in supply commitments.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"Supply commitments are not the guarantees or credit support the question counts.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:e115\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA's days sales outstanding is extending to 60 days.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"yes\",\"bears\":\"no\",\"why\":\"The collection period does not indicate the amount of NVIDIA's guarantees or credit support.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:e219\",\"fact\":\"Aolani plans 22,000 NVIDIA GPUs across two factories in early 2027.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The planned scale of a partner's deployment makes a larger support amount more plausible, favoring higher answers; the support terms remain undisclosed.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:e233\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA estimates Rubin revenue opportunity at $40 billion per gigawatt.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"It suggests the scale of infrastructure NVIDIA supports, making higher answers more plausible; revenue opportunity is not the amount guaranteed.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:e389\",\"fact\":\"One gigawatt of compute costs an estimated $50 billion to $60 billion.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The estimate suggests nearly two gigawatts could involve substantial financing, making higher answers more plausible; it does not specify NVIDIA's support amount.\"}\n{\"item\":\"v1:e488\",\"fact\":\"NVIDIA estimates Rubin revenue opportunity at $40 billion per gigawatt.\",\"in_article\":\"yes\",\"already_shown\":\"no\",\"bears\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"It suggests the scale of infrastructure NVIDIA supports, making higher answers more plausible; revenue opportunity is not the amount guaranteed.\"}"}}
